---
/* The claim and the check — for "Never believe the agent".
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One drawing of the article's whole mechanism: the agent's claim goes in one
   side, the battery of real commands sits in the middle, and there are three
   ways out — and only three. Green is the only path to a confirmed
   checkpoint; red hands the checkpoint to another session; and the third lane
   is the one the article exists for: a gate that never ran at all, dying on
   the way up with an exit status the command did not choose. An agent
   reporting that its tests pass would sail straight past all three.

   Same idiom as FigureLoop: panes, hairlines, mono labels, arrows that are
   characters, semantic colour for status only. */
---

<figure class="fig-gates">
  <div
    class="flow"
    role="img"
    aria-label="The agent claims done. The claim goes to the gate battery — real commands with real exit codes, and nobody asks the agent. Three ways out: exit code zero confirms the checkpoint; a non-zero exit leaves the checkpoint open for another session; and a crashed gate never ran its command at all — an exit status the command did not choose."
  >
    <div class="row" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="box claim">agent: done</span>
      <span class="arrow">→</span>
      <span class="box battery">gate battery — real commands, real exit codes</span>
    </div>

    <ul class="outcomes" aria-hidden="true">
      <li>
        <span class="lane green">exit zero</span>
        <span class="what">the engine confirms — done <span class="ok">✓</span> — and only this path closes a checkpoint</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lane red">exit non-zero</span>
        <span class="what">the checkpoint stays open; another session takes it, with the gate's output in hand</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="lane crashed">crashed</span>
        <span class="what">the command's own logic never ran — died on the way up, in an eyeblink, wearing an exit status it did not choose</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The claim and the check are different programs, which is the article's one
    rule. The third lane is why: a gate that crashes before its command runs
    fails honestly — and every claim of "my tests pass" would have sailed
    straight past it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig-gates {
    margin: 0;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-l);
    inline-size: min(100%, var(--measure-wide));
  }

  .flow {
    background: var(--mantle);
    border: var(--rule);
    padding: var(--space-m) var(--space-m) var(--space-s);
  }

  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .box {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    border: var(--rule);
    background: var(--base);
    padding-block: var(--space-2xs);
    padding-inline: var(--space-xs);
    color: var(--text);
  }

  /* The claim is quiet on purpose: it is an input to the machine, not a
     status. The battery is the pane's subject. */
  .box.claim {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .arrow {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .outcomes {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-m);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .outcomes li {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-xs);
    border-block-start: var(--rule);
    padding-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .lane {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: var(--text-micro);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    flex: 0 0 8rem;
  }

  /* Status colours doing status work — the only decoration this site allows
     them. The crashed lane wears peach, the Face's own role for work that
     stopped rather than failed. */
  .lane.green {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  .lane.red {
    color: var(--red);
  }

  .lane.crashed {
    color: var(--peach);
  }

  .what {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
    flex: 1 1 16rem;
  }

  .ok {
    color: var(--green);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
